{"bill":{"id":"119-s-1813","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"1813","title":"High-Quality Charter Schools Act","introducedDate":"2025-05-20","latestActionDate":"2026-03-19","latestActionText":"Committee on Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions. Hearings held.","policyArea":"Taxation","subjects":["Charitable contributions","Elementary and secondary education","Income tax credits","Student aid and college costs","Tax-exempt organizations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S001184","cosponsorBioguideIds":["C001075","T000278","S001217"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"High-Quality Charter Schools Act This bill establishes a federal tax credit for 75% of the qualified contributions of cash or market securities made by an individual to an eligible charter school organization to create or expand the charter schools operated or managed by the organization. (Limitations apply.) The bill limits the tax credit to the greater of $5,000 or 10% of the individual’s adjusted gross income. The tax credit is not refundable, but any amount of the tax credit that exceeds an individual’s tax liability for the tax year may be carried forward for up to five tax years. Further, the bill establishes an annual $5 billion volume cap (which must be increased under certain circumstances) from which $10 million must be distributed to each state for allocation of the tax credit to individuals residing in the state. The remaining amount of the volume cap must be allocated to individuals on a first-come, first-serve basis. The bill requires the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) to develop a system for tracking qualified contributions in real time. Under the bill, an eligible charter school organization generally is required to spend all of the qualified contributions (less reasonable administrative expenses) within a certain amount of time. If the IRS determines that an organization has failed to meet such spending requirements, the tax credit is not allowed for contributions to the organization for one year after the date of the determination.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/1813","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s1813.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:11.235Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-1813","plainSummary":"This bill would create a federal tax credit equal to 75% of cash or securities donated to eligible charter school organizations that create or expand charter schools. Individual taxpayers could claim up to $5,000 or 10% of their adjusted gross income, whichever is greater, with unused credits carried forward for five years. The bill sets a $5 billion annual cap on total credits, with $10 million allocated to each state and the remainder distributed first-come, first-served, and requires charter organizations to spend contributions within a specified timeframe or lose eligibility.","industries":[{"industry":"education","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly subsidizes contributions to charter school organizations, expanding their funding sources and operational capacity."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"The bill allows donations of market securities, which may affect how financial institutions handle charitable transfers of investment assets."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"14cd277cfd5dcd25f76dec9a","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:35:11.621Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would set up a federal tax credit for 75% of the qualified contributions of cash or market securities made by an individual to an eligible charter school organization to create or expand the charter schools operated or managed by the organization.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who files a tax return. Anyone who pays federal tax.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in your tax return. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary",